Yes, we made a mistake in code organization which, in turn,
led to making a mistake in the Makefile.  TEXTCOLOR uses defines
now present in decl.h, consequently we declared in the Make file
a dependency of monst.c and objects.c on decl.h -- and this is the 
source of the loop.  We fixed that problem, in part, by moving the
TEXTCOLOR defines out of decl.h, and we are discussing now the best
way of getting the fix to you.  For the time being, please delete
from the Makefiles the dependency declaration of monst.c and 
objects.c on decl.h.

Thanks to all of you who sent us msgs, both private and public, about
this problem.  The mistake slipped by simply because our compilers
did not complain.

Some people mention problems with applying the patches.  Such problems
may result from stop-gap patches which were not reversed before
applying the Patchlevel diffs.  Please save the stop-gap patches and do
the reversal before applying Patchlevel diffs.  We assume, for instance,
that you save personalized files (Makefile, config.h, etc.) and that you
replace them with the latest mint-patchlevel files before applying the
next patchlevel diffs.  If you do these things, there is no reason for
patch failure (we test patchlevels diffs, and so does Bill Randle)
except for a defective patch program.

If you do all of the above and still experience patch failure, we suggest
that you refresh your code from the uunet.uu.net archive.  The original
3.0 release and all subsequent official patches are archived in the area:
comp.sources.games/volume7/nethack3.  A patchlevel 4 code is also
available in linc's pub/NH3.0 (it does NOT contain the stop-gap patch
circulated a couple of days ago, NOR the temporary fix to the Makefile
suggested above).  If you are refreshing your code from linc's pub/NH3.0,
please read the README files first.

